{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XcibfTKjbC3E8cJv26ynp4","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/harness/log.md:7:- The gates were verified on macOS with Homebrew Ruby 4.0.0, SQLite3, Node 25 and `svn` installed. `bin/harness check-fast` took about 40 s (RuboCop on 1099 files, Stylelint, 3383 unit and helper tests, 0 failures, 92 skips). `bin/harness check` took about 3 minutes (6296 tests, 0 failures, 131 skips, plus 1 autoload test).\ndocs/harness/log.md:12:- Finding: on a new SQLite3 database, `bin/rails db:prepare` fails every time at migration 072 (`db/migrate/072_add_enumerations_position.rb`) with `no such column: enumerations.position`. The migration skips `add_column` when `Enumeration.column_names` already lists `position`, so stale column information makes it fail. `bin/rails db:create db:migrate` passes every time (CI uses it too, through `ci:setup`). `bin/harness setup` therefore uses `db:create db:migrate`. The migration was not changed; a human should decide whether to fix it upstream.\ndocs/harness/log.md:19:The harness uses Rake, the task runner Redmine already has: new tasks in the root Rakefile, plus a bin/harness wrapper that picks a supported Ruby and bootstraps config/database.yml for SQLite3 before bundle install. From a clean shell, `bin/harness setup` works. `bin/harness check-fast` passes in about 40 s (RuboCop, Stylelint, 3383 unit and helper tests, 0 failures). `bin/harness check` passes in about 3 minutes (6296 tests plus the autoload test, 0 failures). No test was skipped, deleted or excluded, and no product code changed. One finding: `db:prepare` always fails at migration 072 on a new database, so setup uses `db:create db:migrate`. Docs, three ADRs (two inferred), four skills, AGENTS.md, a CI workflow running uw-harness verify, a pre-push hook, compose.yaml, .env.example and .tool-versions were added. Seven open questions (deploy, observability, contribution process, CI, default database, skipped tests) are TODO blocks.\ndocs/harness/log.md:21:**Assessment.** Redmine is a flexible, open-source project management web application built with Ruby on Rails. Stack: Ruby (>= 3.3, < 4.1; CI tests 3.3, 3.4, 4.0), JavaScript (Stimulus controllers via importmap), CSS, Perl (extra/svn/Redmine.pm), Rails 8.1.4, Hotwire Stimulus (stimulus-rails) with importmap-rails, Propshaft asset pipeline, Doorkeeper (OAuth2 provider), Minitest with mocha, Capybara + Selenium (system tests), RuboCop (+performance, +rails), Stylelint, Puma (server in tests and install docs). The full assessment stays in `.git/uw-harness/assessment.md` on the machine that ran uw-harness.\ndocs/harness/log.md:25:- The Gemfile reads config/database.yml (git-ignored) to choose database gems: without that file, bundle install warns and no database adapter is installed, so setup and every gate fail.\ndocs/harness/log.md:63:**First task to try with the harness:** Add a test for the /up health check route. config/routes.rb exposes GET /up (rails/health#show) for load balancers and uptime monitors, but no test in test/ covers it. Add a routing test and an integration test, so that a change to the routes or to authentication filters cannot break the health check without a failing test.\ndocs/harness/log.md:108:- local-env: On a new SQLite3 database, bin/rails db:prepare fails every time at db/migrate/072_add_enumerations_position.rb (no such column: enumerations.position). The migration skips add_column when Enumeration.column_names already lists the column. bin/harness setup uses db:create db:migrate instead, which passes every time. The migration was not changed: a human should decide whether to fix it upstream.\ndocs/harness/log.md:111:- ci: The uw-harness package is not published on npm (npx -y uw-harness returned 404 here), so the new harness.yml workflow will fail until it is published. The gates were checked with the local build of uw-harness (check passes) and run directly from a clean shell.\nconfig/ini